 daily record. Since 2000, Cedar Rapids has set <strong>61 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 74 that chance alone would give, and <strong>73 record highs</strong>, close to the 74 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (57). Cedar Rapids' all-time high is 110\u00b0F, on July 6, 1911.","t_chg":"Cedar Rapids' nights show no clear change. Its days cooled over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 130 complete years: Cedar Rapids' average low shows no clear change: +0.01\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.06 to +0.09) that includes zero. Cedar Rapids' average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.18 to \u22120.01), about \u22121.3\u00b0F since 1893. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in none; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Cedar Rapids has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Cedar Rapids' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.43 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.76), about +5.7 inches since 1893. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 35.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1993 (60.46 in); the driest, 1910 (18.74 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}